{- | Gate for import-clause order invariance over
   `test/import_order_fixtures`.

   A golden cannot catch an over-widening. A golden pins the output the
   compiler produced, so a rule that is too permissive has its permissive
   answer recorded and then defended. The check for an order-dependence bug
   is a permutation differential instead: permute the input along the
   suspect axis and require the answer to be invariant. That needs no ground
   truth, which is what lets it see a widening nobody has written down the
   right answer for.

   The axis is the entry module's import-clause order. Reordering imports is
   cosmetic — every formatter and every reviewer treats it that way — so a
   program whose meaning depends on it is wrong whatever the right answer
   turns out to be.

   The unit is a directory, not a file, because every import-order defect on
   the tracker is multi-module by nature. Each case directory declares its
   entry module in a `case.txt`.

   # What must be equal across orderings

   Each ordering is reduced to a one-line signature, and it is the signature
   that must be invariant:

   `check=<exit>;codes=<sorted,comma>;schemes=<dump>;run=<exit>:<stdout>;build=<exit>/<exec-exit>:<stdout>`

   - `check=` is `medaka check --json`'s exit code, kept exactly, because a
     change from 1 to 2 is also a change.
   - `codes=` is every `"code"` in that JSON, sorted and as a multiset.
     Sorted because diagnostics are emitted in source order and permutation
     moves source lines. A multiset because one `T-TYPE-MISMATCH` and two
     are genuinely different observations: an exit-code-only criterion is
     blind to the cascading second code one ordering of
     `733-ambiguous-ctor-reject-cascade-order-dependent` emits, and a
     set-not-multiset one is blind to a pair.
   - `schemes=` is plain `check`'s scheme dump for the user's own top-level
     bindings, selected by FORMAT (`<name> : <type>` anchored at column 0)
     and never by stream: resolve-stage diagnostics go to stderr and
     type-stage ones to stdout, so the stream does not discriminate, and
     stdout carries locations, message text and the harness's own temporary
     directory, none of which may enter a signature. It is the only
     observable that can see "exit 0, same printed value, but the binding was
     inferred at a different type" — what a different declaration winning
     looks like when the values happen to coincide.
   - `run=` and `build=` are two observations of the backend, not two of the
     front end, which `run` and `build` share. They can disagree: two engines
     have picked opposite winners for the same source. Both are graded.

   Deliberately excluded, each because comparing it manufactures a false red:
   diagnostic message TEXT (`R-AMBIGUOUS-CTOR` names the colliding modules in
   import-clause order, on purpose, and the corpus keeps that program as
   `control-ambiguous-ctor-reject-invariant` so the exclusion is itself
   graded — the row still fails if the verdict or the code becomes
   order-dependent); diagnostic RANGES (permuting clauses of unequal line
   count moves every line below them); `run`/`build` STDERR (runtime panics
   are not uniformly location-free and the build log embeds the source path);
   the ORDER of diagnostics, hence the sort; and the emitted IR.

   # The emitter-verdict arm

   A fourth driver, graded in its own space against its own sidecar ledger,
   `test/EMITTER-VERDICT-LEDGER.txt`. The raw emitter binary can exit 0 and
   emit a complete, linkable program for an entry `check`/`run`/`build` all
   reject, because the one `failWith` on a typecheck diagnostic on that path
   matches a singleton module list and every import-bearing entry — the whole
   of this corpus — falls to a wildcard clause returning no diagnostics.

   Only the emitter's exit code and whether it wrote non-empty IR are
   observed: never the IR text, which moves on every compiler change, and
   never its stderr, which embeds absolute temporary paths. The observed
   AGREE/DISAGREE classification is computed, never transcribed.

   Keeping it separate is load-bearing. Folding an `emit=` cell into the
   signature above would re-cut every row already pinned in
   `test/IMPORT-ORDER-LEDGER.txt`, and `test/MUST-FAIL-NOT-PINNABLE.txt`'s
   `1667` row names this arm as the permanent guard for that issue.

   # Scope limits

   Only the ENTRY module's clauses are permuted; a defect decided by an
   imported module's clause order is not covered here, and nothing else
   covers it either. Clauses are permuted among their own original slots, so
   a clause never moves across a declaration, and an order sensitivity that
   needs an import to move past a `data` or an `impl` is out of scope by
   construction. All `n!` orderings are tried when `n <= 4`; above that the
   plan falls back to identity, reversal and every adjacent transposition and
   the case is reported PARTIAL, so a shrunken permutation set can never look
   like a full one.

   # The ledgers

   `test/IMPORT-ORDER-LEDGER.txt` carries the cases whose answer does depend
   on import order today, each naming its open issue and pinning every
   distinct signature it produces. All four directions are graded: an
   unledgered case producing two signatures fails (a new bug); a ledgered
   case collapsing to one fails, naming the issue to close (the drain); a
   ledgered case whose signatures moved fails; and a ledger row naming a case
   that does not exist fails (rot). Read that file's own header for what a
   reviewer must demand before accepting a new row.

   Both ledgers are read offline. Whether a ledgered issue is still OPEN is
   the half a gate cannot check — it runs on every dev box and in a required
   CI shard, where a rate limit or a fork's restricted token must never block
   a merge — and lives in `test/must_fail_census.sh`, which has the API.

   # Vehicle

   Runs under `medaka test --native`: it spawns subprocesses and reads and
   writes files, none of which the interpreter binds.

   The verdict census is rendered into the failure message rather than
   printed, because `medaka test --native` parses a test body's stdout back
   as a sentinel transcript and absorbs anything the body prints on its own
   account. The three axes — structural, order and emitter-verdict — are
   graded in one `test` declaration because each ordering is spawned once and
   feeds all three; the failure message names which axis each row failed on,
   so an emitter-verdict regression is legible on its own. -}

import compiler_cli_test_support.{boundedInTree, emitterBin}
import fs.{fixtureDirs, fixtureFiles, mkdirAll}
import io.{runVerb}
import list.{
  drop, errs, indexed, lookup, mapWithIndex, nub, oks, permutations, range,
  reverse, somes, sort, zip
}
import path.{basename}
import string.{
  contains, isAlpha, isAlphaNum, isDigit, join, lines, padRight, replaceAll,
  split, startsWith, stripPrefix, stripSuffix, toChars, trim, trimLeft, unlines
}
import test.{Expectation, fail, pass}
import test_process.{medakaBin, medakaRoot, missingUnits, scratchDir}

-- Everything is located through MEDAKA_ROOT rather than the working
-- directory: a gate runner exports it and inherits whatever cwd its caller
-- happened to be in.
corpus : <IO> String
corpus = "\{medakaRoot}/test/import_order_fixtures"

orderLedgerPath : <IO> String
orderLedgerPath = "\{medakaRoot}/test/IMPORT-ORDER-LEDGER.txt"

emitLedgerPath : <IO> String
emitLedgerPath = "\{medakaRoot}/test/EMITTER-VERDICT-LEDGER.txt"

{- | The ceiling on one spawned invocation, in seconds, sized to cover a
   `medaka build` and the `clang` it drives.

   A regression that makes one ordering loop has to surface as a failing row
   rather than as a hung CI job. -}
perSpawnSeconds : Int
perSpawnSeconds = 120

-- # Chunking and permuting the entry module

{- | One top-level chunk of an entry module: a line with a non-whitespace
   character in column 0 together with every blank or indented line that
   follows it.

   The offside rule puts every top-level declaration in column 0, so this is
   what makes a multi-line `import m.{ ... }` move as one unit. -}
data Chunk = Chunk { isImport : Bool, text : String }

{- | An entry module split into chunks, with the positions of its import
   chunks and whatever trailing newline the file ended with.

   The ending is carried separately so a rewritten file is byte-identical to
   the original wherever the permutation is the identity. -}
data Source = Source { chunks : List Chunk, slots : List Int, ending : String }

startsChunk : String -> Bool
startsChunk piece =
  piece /= "" && not (startsWith " " piece) && not (startsWith "\t" piece)

-- Matches `import m…` and `export import m…`, the two forms a top-level
-- import clause takes.
importHead : String -> Bool
importHead line =
  let bare = match stripPrefix "export" line
    Some rest =>
      if startsWith " " rest || startsWith "\t" rest then
        trimLeft rest
      else
        line
    None => line
  startsWith "import " bare || startsWith "import\t" bare

addPiece : List Chunk -> String -> List Chunk
addPiece acc piece =
  if startsChunk piece then
    Chunk { isImport = importHead piece, text = piece } :: acc
  else match acc
    [] => [Chunk { isImport = False, text = piece }]
    c :: rest => Chunk { c | text = "\{c.text}\n\{piece}" } :: rest

sourceOf : String -> Source
sourceOf src =
  let (body, ending) = match stripSuffix "\n" src
    Some b => (b, "\n")
    None => (src, "")
  let chunks = reverse (fold addPiece [] (split "\n" body))
  Source {
    chunks = chunks,
    slots = map fst (filter (e => (snd e).isImport) (indexed chunks)),
    ending = ending,
  }

{- | The orderings to try for `n` import clauses, and whether that set is
   partial. -}
data Plan = Plan { allOrders : List (List Int), shrunken : Bool }

ordersFor : Int -> Plan
ordersFor n =
  if n <= 4 then
    Plan { allOrders = permutations (range 0 n), shrunken = False }
  else
    Plan {
      allOrders =
        range 0 n
          :: reverse (range 0 n)
          :: map (i => swapAdjacent i n) (range 0 (n - 1)),
      shrunken = True,
    }

swapAdjacent : Int -> Int -> List Int
swapAdjacent i n =
  map (j => if j == i then i + 1 else if j == i + 1 then i else j) (range 0 n)

{- | The entry module rewritten so that its import clauses appear in `order`,
   each moved into one of the slots the clauses already occupied. -}
permutedText : Source -> List Int -> String
permutedText src order =
  let original = map (i => src.chunks[i].text) src.slots
  let placed = zip src.slots (map (w => original[w]) order)
  let render =
    e => match lookup (fst e) placed
      Some t => t
      None => (snd e).text
  "\{join "\n" (map render (indexed src.chunks))}\{src.ending}"

-- # The signature

escapeCell : String -> String
escapeCell s =
  replaceAll
    "\n"
    "\\n"
    (replaceAll
      "\t"
      "\\t"
      (replaceAll ";" "\\s" (replaceAll "|" "\\p" (replaceAll "\\" "\\\\" s))))

-- Every `"code":"…"` in `check --json`'s output, sorted, as a multiset.
codesIn : String -> String
codesIn text =
  join "," (sort (map upToQuote (drop 1 (split "\"code\":\"" text))))

upToQuote : String -> String
upToQuote piece = match split "\"" piece
  h :: _ => h
  [] => piece

{- | The scheme lines of plain `check`'s stdout, selected by their format:
   an identifier anchored at column 0 followed by ` : `.

   A diagnostic line cannot match — it begins with a path, a digit or the
   `  |` gutter. A scheme wrapped onto a continuation line loses its tail,
   which is a known limit rather than a hazard: the tail is dropped
   identically in every ordering. -}
schemeDump : String -> String
schemeDump out = unlines (filter schemeLine (lines out))

schemeLine : String -> Bool
schemeLine line = match split " : " line
  ident :: _ :: _ => identifierText ident
  _ => False

identifierText : String -> Bool
identifierText s = match toList (toChars s)
  [] => False
  c :: rest => (isAlpha c || c == '_') && all identifierChar rest

identifierChar : Char -> Bool
identifierChar c = isAlphaNum c || c == '_' || c == '\''

{- | One ordering's graded observations: its signature, its emitter-verdict
   signature, and whether its `build` produced a binary. -}
data Trial = Trial { sigText : String, emitText : String, madeBinary : Bool }

verb : List String -> <Exec "_", IO> Result String (Int, String, String)
verb args = boundedInTree perSpawnSeconds medakaBin args

executed : Int ->
  String ->
  <Exec "_", FileRead "_", IO> Result String (Option (Int, String))
executed 0 binPath =
  if fileExists binPath then
    map
      ((code, out, _) => Some (code, out))
      (boundedInTree perSpawnSeconds binPath [])
  else
    Ok None
executed _ _ = Ok None

signatureFor : String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  <Exec "_", FileRead "_", IO> Result String (String, Bool)
signatureFor work entry binPath =
  let path = "\{work}/\{entry}"
  match verb ["check", "--json", path]
    Err e => Err "`medaka check --json` could not run: \{e}"
    Ok (checkCode, jsonOut, jsonErr) => match verb ["check", path]
      Err e => Err "`medaka check` could not run: \{e}"
      Ok (_, checkOut, _) => match verb ["run", path]
        Err e => Err "`medaka run` could not run: \{e}"
        Ok (runCode, runOut, _) => match verb ["build", path, "-o", binPath]
          Err e => Err "`medaka build` could not run: \{e}"
          Ok (buildCode, _, _) => match executed buildCode binPath
            Err e => Err "the built binary could not run: \{e}"
            Ok ran =>
              let execCode = match ran
                Some (c, _) => intToString c
                None => "-"
              let execOut = match ran
                Some (_, o) => escapeCell o
                None => ""
              let codes = codesIn (jsonOut ++ jsonErr)
              let schemes = escapeCell (schemeDump checkOut)
              let checkCells =
                "check=\{intToString checkCode};codes=\{codes};schemes=\{schemes}"
              let runCells = "run=\{intToString runCode}:\{escapeCell runOut}"
              let buildCells =
                "build=\{intToString buildCode}/\{execCode}:\{execOut}"
              Ok ("\{checkCells};\{runCells};\{buildCells}", isSome ran)

{- | The raw emitter's own verdict on one ordering.

   The extra `stdlib` root gives it the same root set `check`/`run`/`build`
   get through `MEDAKA_ROOT`: without it any case importing a non-`core`
   stdlib module exits 1 "unknown module" for the emitter alone, which would
   classify as DISAGREE for a root-set reason that has nothing to do with a
   compiler defect. -}
emitVerdictFor : String -> String -> <Exec "_", IO> Result String String
emitVerdictFor work entry =
  let roots = [
    "\{medakaRoot}/stdlib/runtime.mdk",
    "\{medakaRoot}/stdlib/core.mdk",
    "\{work}/\{entry}",
    work,
    "\{medakaRoot}/stdlib",
  ]
  match boundedInTree perSpawnSeconds emitterBin roots
    Err e => Err "the raw emitter could not run: \{e}"
    Ok (code, out, _) =>
      let ir = if out == "" then "noir" else "ir"
      Ok "emit=\{intToString code}:\{ir}"

-- The `check=` cell of a signature, which is what the emitter-verdict
-- classification is computed against.
checkCell : String -> String
checkCell sig = match split ";" sig
  h :: _ => h
  [] => sig

-- # Running one case

{- | One case directory's graded result. `broken` is a structural failure —
   no `case.txt`, an entry that is not a file, too few import clauses, a
   spawn that could not start — which has no signatures of its own. -}
data Case = Case {
  unit : String,
  clauseCount : Int,
  orderingCount : Int,
  partial : Bool,
  sigs : List String,
  emitSigs : List String,
  anyBuilt : Bool,
  anyEmitted : Bool,
  broken : String,
}

brokenCase : String -> String -> Case
brokenCase unit why = Case {
  unit = unit,
  clauseCount = 0,
  orderingCount = 0,
  partial = False,
  sigs = [],
  emitSigs = [],
  anyBuilt = False,
  anyEmitted = False,
  broken = why,
}

specField : String -> String -> String
specField key spec = match somes (map (stripPrefix "\{key}:") (lines spec))
  v :: _ => trim v
  [] => ""

{- | Copies a case directory whole and asserts the copy landed everything.

   `cp -a` rather than a per-file copy because a case directory may hold a
   nested-module subdirectory, and a truncated copy would truncate every
   ordering identically, which the invariance check cannot see. -}
stageCase : String ->
  String ->
  <Exec "_", FileRead "_", FileWrite "_", IO> Result String Unit
stageCase dir work = match mkdirAll work
  Err e => Err "could not create \{work}: \{e}"
  Ok _ => match runVerb "cp" ["-a", "\{dir}/.", "\{work}/"]
    Err e => Err "could not copy \{dir}: \{e}"
    Ok (code, _, cpErr) =>
      if code /= 0 then
        Err "`cp -a \{dir}/. \{work}/` exited \{intToString code}: \{cpErr}"
      else match (fixtureFiles dir, fixtureFiles work)
        (Err e, _) => Err "could not enumerate \{dir}: \{e}"
        (_, Err e) => Err "could not enumerate \{work}: \{e}"
        (Ok srcFiles, Ok dstFiles) =>
          if length srcFiles == length dstFiles then
            Ok ()
          else
            Err
              "case-directory copy truncated: \{intToString (length srcFiles)} file(s) in, \{intToString (length dstFiles)} out"

trialFor : String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  Source ->
  Int ->
  List Int ->
  <Exec "_", FileRead "_", FileWrite "_", IO> Result String Trial
trialFor caseScratch dir entry src k order =
  let work = "\{caseScratch}/p\{intToString k}"
  match stageCase dir work
    Err e => Err e
    Ok _ => match writeFile "\{work}/\{entry}" (permutedText src order)
      Err e => Err "could not write the permuted entry: \{e}"
      Ok _ => match signatureFor work entry "\{work}/.bin"
        Err e => Err e
        Ok (sig, built) =>
          map
            (emitted => Trial {
              sigText = sig,
              emitText = "\{checkCell sig};\{emitted}",
              madeBinary = built,
            })
            (emitVerdictFor work entry)

runCase : String -> String -> <Exec "_", FileRead "_", FileWrite "_", IO> Case
runCase scratch unit =
  let dir = "\{corpus}/\{unit}"
  match readFile "\{dir}/case.txt"
    Err e => brokenCase unit "no readable case.txt: \{e}"
    Ok spec =>
      let entry = specField "entry" spec
      if entry == "" then
        brokenCase unit "case.txt declares no entry"
      else match readFile "\{dir}/\{entry}"
        Err e =>
          brokenCase
            unit
            "case.txt names entry \{debug entry}, which is not a readable file here: \{e}"
        Ok raw =>
          let src = sourceOf raw
          let n = length src.slots
          if n < 2 then
            brokenCase
              unit
              "needs at least 2 top-level import clauses, found \{intToString n}"
          else
            let plan = ordersFor n
            let outcomes =
              mapWithIndex
                (trialFor "\{scratch}/\{unit}" dir entry src)
                plan.allOrders
            match errs outcomes
              e :: _ => brokenCase unit e
              [] =>
                let trials = oks outcomes
                Case {
                  unit = unit,
                  clauseCount = n,
                  orderingCount = length trials,
                  partial = plan.shrunken,
                  sigs = sort (nub (map (t => t.sigText) trials)),
                  emitSigs = sort (nub (map (t => t.emitText) trials)),
                  anyBuilt = any (t => t.madeBinary) trials,
                  anyEmitted =
                    any (t => contains "emit=0:ir" t.emitText) trials,
                  broken = "",
                }

-- # The ledgers

{- | One ledger row: the case it names, its classification field (an issue
   number in the order ledger, an AGREE/DISAGREE class in the emitter one),
   its reason where the format carries one, and every signature it pins. -}
data Row = Row {
  rowCase : String,
  tag : String,
  reason : String,
  pins : List String,
}

ledgerComment : String -> Bool
ledgerComment line = trim line == "" || startsWith "#" (trimLeft line)

{- | The rows of a `|`-separated ledger, with the pinned signatures starting
   at field `pinAt` and everything before them read as metadata.

   A line with fewer than three fields is not a row, matching what both
   ledger files' own headers require of one. -}
ledgerRows : Int -> String -> List Row
ledgerRows pinAt text =
  somes (map (rowOf pinAt) (filter (l => not (ledgerComment l)) (lines text)))

rowOf : Int -> String -> Option Row
rowOf pinAt line =
  let fields = map trim (split "|" line)
  if length fields < 3 then
    None
  else
    Some Row {
      rowCase = fields[0],
      tag = fields[1],
      reason = if pinAt >= 3 then fields[2] else "",
      pins = sort (nub (filter (/= "") (drop pinAt fields))),
    }

rowFor : String -> List Row -> Option Row
rowFor unit rows = match filter (r => r.rowCase == unit) rows
  r :: _ => Some r
  [] => None

-- The digits of an issue field spelled `#<digits>…`, or `None`.
issueDigits : String -> Option String
issueDigits tag = match stripPrefix "#" tag
  None => None
  Some rest => match toList (toChars rest)
    d :: _ => if isDigit d then Some rest else None
    [] => None

-- # Grading

failLine : String -> String -> String
failLine unit why = "  \{padRight 52 ' ' unit} \{why}"

sigListing : List String -> String
sigListing sigs = join "" (map (s => "\n         \{s}") sigs)

{- | The order axis for one case: invariant unless a ledger row licenses the
   divergence, and then only the exact divergence that row pins. -}
gradeOrder : List Row -> Case -> Option String
gradeOrder rows c =
  if c.broken /= "" then
    Some (failLine c.unit c.broken)
  else match rowFor c.unit rows
    None =>
      if length c.sigs == 1 then
        None
      else
        Some
          (failLine
            c.unit
            "\{intToString c.orderingCount} ordering(s) produced \{intToString (length c.sigs)} DISTINCT signatures — the answer depends on import order. Either FIX it, or, if it is a known OPEN bug, add a row to test/IMPORT-ORDER-LEDGER.txt after reading that file's header:\{sigListing c.sigs}")
    Some row => gradeLedgered c row

gradeLedgered : Case -> Row -> Option String
gradeLedgered c row = match issueDigits row.tag
  None =>
    Some
      (failLine
        c.unit
        "ledger row's issue field is \{debug row.tag} — it must be #<number> of an OPEN issue")
  Some num =>
    if not (startsWith "\{num}-" c.unit) then
      Some
        (failLine
          c.unit
          "ledgered against \{row.tag} but the directory is not named \"\{num}-…\"")
    else if length row.pins < 2 then
      Some
        (failLine
          c.unit
          "ledger row pins \{intToString (length row.pins)} signature(s); a row asserts a DIVERGENCE, so it needs at least 2")
    else if length c.sigs == 1 then
      Some
        (failLine
          c.unit
          "DRAINED — \{row.tag} no longer diverges under import-clause permutation. Close it, delete the row, and KEEP the fixture as the regression guard. Observed:\{sigListing c.sigs}")
    else if row.pins == c.sigs then
      None
    else
      Some
        (failLine
          c.unit
          "KNOWN-BAD \{row.tag}: the divergence MOVED. Re-derive the right answer before re-pinning; a ledger row is evidence, not a knob.\n       pinned:\{sigListing row.pins}\n       observed:\{sigListing c.sigs}")

{- | Whether the raw emitter's accept/reject verdict differs from `check`'s
   in any ordering — computed from the observed pairs, never transcribed. -}
emitClass : List String -> String
emitClass sigs = if any emitDisagrees sigs then "DISAGREE" else "AGREE"

emitDisagrees : String -> Bool
emitDisagrees sig = match split ";" sig
  checkPart :: emitPart :: _ =>
    let accepted = (== "0")
    accepted (fieldAfter "check=" checkPart)
      /= accepted (upToColon (fieldAfter "emit=" emitPart))
  _ => False

fieldAfter : String -> String -> String
fieldAfter prefix s = match stripPrefix prefix s
  Some rest => rest
  None => s

upToColon : String -> String
upToColon s = match split ":" s
  h :: _ => h
  [] => s

gradeEmit : List Row -> Case -> Option String
gradeEmit rows c =
  if c.broken /= "" || c.emitSigs == [] then
    None
  else
    let observed = emitClass c.emitSigs
    match rowFor c.unit rows
      None =>
        if observed == "AGREE" then
          None
        else
          Some
            (failLine
              c.unit
              "emitter-verdict: DISAGREE, UNLEDGERED — the raw emitter's accept/reject verdict diverges from check's and nobody has pinned it. Add a row to test/EMITTER-VERDICT-LEDGER.txt. Observed:\{sigListing c.emitSigs}")
      Some row => gradeEmitRow c row observed

gradeEmitRow : Case -> Row -> String -> Option String
gradeEmitRow c row observed =
  if observed == "AGREE" then
    Some
      (failLine
        c.unit
        "emitter-verdict: DRAINED — row present but the emitter now agrees. Delete it from test/EMITTER-VERDICT-LEDGER.txt. Observed:\{sigListing c.emitSigs}")
  else if row.tag /= "DISAGREE" then
    Some
      (failLine
        c.unit
        "emitter-verdict: row classification says \{debug row.tag} but observed is DISAGREE")
  else if row.reason == "" || row.reason == "-" then
    Some
      (failLine
        c.unit
        "emitter-verdict: row has no reason — a row must say why")
  else if row.pins == c.emitSigs then
    None
  else
    Some
      (failLine
        c.unit
        "emitter-verdict: pinned set differs from observed.\n       pinned:\{sigListing row.pins}\n       observed:\{sigListing c.emitSigs}")

{- | An arm that graded nothing must not report green.

   If `medaka build` failed for every ordering of every case, every signature
   would carry the same `build=1/-:` and every case would still compare
   equal — a green gate whose third verb graded nothing. The same rule one
   level down for the emitter arm: no `emit=0:ir` anywhere means the emitter
   never once succeeded, which is indistinguishable from the arm never
   running. -}
witnessFailures : List Case -> List String
witnessFailures cases =
  let noBuild =
    if any (c => c.anyBuilt) cases then
      []
    else
      [
        "  the BUILD arm produced no executable anywhere in the corpus — it graded nothing",
      ]
  let noEmit =
    if any (c => c.anyEmitted) cases then
      []
    else
      [
        "  the EMITTER-VERDICT arm never observed emit=0:ir anywhere in the corpus — it graded nothing",
      ]
  noBuild ++ noEmit

-- The permutation census, so a red run says how much was actually evaluated
-- and which cases ran a shrunken ordering set.
census : List Case -> String
census cases =
  let orderings = sum (map (c => c.orderingCount) cases)
  let partials = map (c => c.unit) (filter (c => c.partial) cases)
  let partialNote = if partials == [] then "none" else join ", " partials
  "\{intToString orderings} ordering(s) over \{intToString (length cases)} case(s); PARTIAL: \{partialNote}"

section : String -> List String -> List String
section _ [] = []
section what failures = "\{what}:" :: failures

summarize : List Case -> List String -> Expectation
summarize _ [] = pass
summarize cases blocks = fail "\{census cases}\n\{unlines blocks}"

-- # The units

corpusUnits : <FileRead "_", IO> Result String (List String)
corpusUnits = map (ds => sort (map basename ds)) (fixtureDirs corpus)

readLedger : String -> Int -> <FileRead "_", IO> Result String (List Row)
readLedger path pinAt = match readFile path
  Err e => Err "could not read \{path}: \{e}"
  Ok text => Ok (ledgerRows pinAt text)

test "every case declares an entry with at least two import clauses" =
  match corpusUnits
    Err e => fail "\{corpus}: \{e}"
    Ok units => match somes (map declarationFailure units)
      [] => pass
      failures =>
        fail
          "\{intToString (length failures)} of \{intToString (length units)} case(s) cannot be permuted:\n\{unlines failures}"

declarationFailure : String -> <FileRead "_", IO> Option String
declarationFailure unit =
  let dir = "\{corpus}/\{unit}"
  match readFile "\{dir}/case.txt"
    Err e => Some (failLine unit "no readable case.txt: \{e}")
    Ok spec =>
      let entry = specField "entry" spec
      if entry == "" then
        Some (failLine unit "case.txt declares no entry")
      else match readFile "\{dir}/\{entry}"
        Err _ =>
          Some
            (failLine
              unit
              "case.txt names entry \{debug entry}, which is not a readable file here")
        Ok raw =>
          let n = length (sourceOf raw).slots
          if n < 2 then
            Some
              (failLine
                unit
                "\{entry} has \{intToString n} top-level import clause(s); permuting needs at least 2")
          else
            None

{- | A ledger entry that outlives its fixture is rot, and rot is how a ledger
   becomes a skip-list. Graded as its own unit because it needs no
   subprocess and its failure is legible on its own. -}
test "neither ledger names a case that is not in the corpus" = match corpusUnits
  Err e => fail "\{corpus}: \{e}"
  Ok units => match (readLedger orderLedgerPath 2, readLedger emitLedgerPath 3)
    (Err e, _) => fail e
    (_, Err e) => fail e
    (Ok orderRows, Ok emitRows) =>
      let stale =
        name rows =>
          map
            (u =>
              "  \{name} names case \{debug u}, which does not exist under test/import_order_fixtures/")
            (missingUnits Some units (map (r => r.rowCase) rows))
      match (stale "test/IMPORT-ORDER-LEDGER.txt" orderRows
        ++ stale "test/EMITTER-VERDICT-LEDGER.txt" emitRows)
        [] => pass
        failures => fail "stale ledger row(s):\n\{unlines failures}"

test "the answer does not depend on import-clause order" = match corpusUnits
  Err e => fail "\{corpus}: \{e}"
  Ok units => match (readLedger orderLedgerPath 2, readLedger emitLedgerPath 3)
    (Err e, _) => fail e
    (_, Err e) => fail e
    (Ok orderRows, Ok emitRows) => match scratchDir
      Err e => fail "could not make a scratch directory: \{e}"
      Ok scratch =>
        let cases = map (runCase scratch) units
        let _ = runVerb "rm" ["-rf", scratch]
        summarize
          cases
          (section
              "the answer depends on import-clause order"
              (somes (map (gradeOrder orderRows) cases))
            ++ section
              "the emitter's verdict is not what the sidecar ledger pins"
              (somes (map (gradeEmit emitRows) cases))
            ++ section "an arm graded nothing" (witnessFailures cases))
